#!/usr/bin/env etcs

# graphical_script.etcs plus a renderer and a layer stack. Same shape, one
# extra module -- but the thread ledger is the part worth reading before
# editing this file, because it is what decides where each line runs.
#
# THREE THREADS, and only one of them is negotiable:
#
#   1. THIS script's own thread -- the first OS thread. GLFW binds window
#      creation and event polling to whichever thread called them, so
#      whatever creates the window here has to be whatever polls it. That
#      is main.Create() below, and then view.RunDemo() (which polls the
#      parent window every frame -- VulkanSurface::RunDemo). Non-negotiable.
#
#   2. A ThreadPool worker -- Window.ProduceEvents. Every produce body is
#      enqueued onto the pool (DEFINE_STREAM_FUNC_PRODUCE, ETCS_API.h), and
#      this one loops until the window closes, so it HOLDS that worker for
#      the window's whole lifetime rather than passing through it.
#
#   3. The detached thread from `detach` -- Window.ConsumeEvents. Consume
#      bodies run inline ("this thread IS the body's thread"), so the
#      detached script thread is the one blocked on the input stream.
#
# The renderer is NOT on its own thread here, and that is the honest
# limitation of this version: Surface.RunDemo drives the frame loop AND
# polls the window, so it must own thread 1. To actually hand rendering off
# -- window keeps the poll loop, frames run somewhere else -- the frame pump
# has to become a stream edge of its own (Surface.ProduceFrames ->
# Surface.ConsumeFrames, RenderProvider.cc's own comment flags this as the
# intended shape) and the script becomes main.Run() plus a second detach.
# See render_script_streamed.etcs for that version.
#
# Run from the ETCS root -- the shader path below is cwd-relative, same
# convention run_website.etcs uses for ./www.

spawn RenderProvider::Instance gpu
gpu.Create()

# Create, not Run: Run would enter the poll loop and never come back, and
# the Surface cannot be built until the window exists -- it takes its
# VkSurfaceKHR from the window's native handle and its swapchain extent
# from the window's size. Ordering here is a real dependency, not style.
spawn WindowProvider::Window main
main.Create(800, 600, 'ETCS RenderProvider')

# Child, not a root spawn: the parent link IS the binding between a surface
# and the window it draws into. Surface.Create walks getParent() for both
# the Window and the Resizable interface pointers.
main.spawn(RenderProvider::Surface view)
view.Create(@gpu, modules/RenderProvider/shaders/)

# The layer stack. Root spawns: a layer has no owner in the scene-graph
# sense, it is just an addressable region of pixels whose RID the thing
# compositing knows. Same drawing verbs as the window surface above --
# the only difference is that these composite on the CPU into their own
# bytes, and that one presents.
spawn RenderProvider::ImageSurface base
base.Create(320, 240)
base.Clear(0.15, 0.18, 0.35, 1.0)
base.DrawRect(20, 20, 120, 80, 0.9, 0.75, 0.15, 1.0)

spawn RenderProvider::ImageSurface overlay
overlay.Create(160, 120)
overlay.Clear(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0)
overlay.DrawRect(0, 0, 160, 60, 0.2, 0.85, 0.4, 0.6)

# Layer onto layer, on the CPU. w/h of 0 mean the source's own size.
base.Blit(@overlay, 140, 130, 0, 0, 0.8)

# Layer onto the screen, on the GPU: same call, different surface.
view.Clear(0.05, 0.05, 0.08, 1.0)
view.Blit(@base, 80, 60, 640, 480, 1.0)
view.Present()

# Input, exactly as graphical_script.etcs does it. Detached AFTER the
# window is live: ProduceEvents spins on IsActive() before registering an
# observer, so detaching it earlier just burns thread 2 waiting.
detach window_events.etcs window=main

# Owns thread 1 for the rest of the run: polls, clears, draws, presents,
# and returns when the frame count runs out or the window is closed.
view.RunDemo(600)

overlay.Delete()
base.Delete()
view.Delete()
gpu.Delete()
main.Delete()
